WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.670
An utterly, wonderous achievement.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:14.040
Astronomers using the Event Horizon Telescope
have taken the first ever photograph of a

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.570
black hole.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:20.730
Here's the story, about a hundred years ago,
Albert Einstein gave us a new description

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:26.260
of the force of gravity in which gravity exerts
its influence through warps and curves in

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.039
the fabric of space and time.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.570
Just a couple of years later, Karl Schwarzschild,
he was a German astronomer who was stationed

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.790
on the Russian front during World War I in
charge of calculating artillery trajectories,

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:45.320
he somehow gets ahold of Einstein's manuscript
and realizes something amazing.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.720
If you take a spherical object and squeeze
it down to sufficiently small size, according

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.550
to Einstein's math the gravitational pull
will be so enormous that nothing will be able

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.550
to escape.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.340
Not even light, and that is what we mean by
a black hole.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.180
Now, when Einstein caught wind of these results,
he didn't believe it.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:13.189
He didn't think these objects would actually
be out there in the universe, and yet in the

00:01:13.189 --> 00:01:19.060
ensuing decades, theoretical developments
began to mount showing that black holes were

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:25.179
the inevitable outcome of massive stars that
had used up their nuclear fuel, undergone

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:30.799
a supernova explosion and the resulting core
would have no ability to withstand the pull

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.329
of gravity when it collapsed down into a black
hole.

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:40.850
And the observational cases also began to
mount studies of the center of our Milky Way

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:47.509
galaxy showed stars whipping around the center
at such an enormous speed that the only explanation

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:52.950
for the object that could exert the powerful
gravity responsible for that motion would

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:59.140
be a very massive black hole, perhaps four
million times the mass of the sun.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.529
And perhaps the most compelling convincing
observational evidence to date actually comes

00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:05.280
from gravitational waves.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.979
When we received the first ripples in the
fabric of space back in 2015, the only explanation

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:16.120
for the pattern of those waves was two black
holes, very distant, that collided, setting

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.420
off a tidal wave in space that we were able
to detect.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:27.370
Now, even with the mounting theoretical and
observational evidence, we never directly

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.190
seen a black hole itself.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.110
And that is what now has changed.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:38.000
Using eight radio telescopes from around the
world, the Event Horizon Telescope, the name

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.510
of this consortium, has now taken the first
ever snapshot of a black hole itself.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.640
And here it is.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.000
The image provides the first direct test of
Einstein's theory and the strong gravitational

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.250
field of a black hole, and as far as we can
tell Einstein was once again comes through

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.140
with flying colors.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:04.510
And in many ways, this image really is an
emblem of the power of the human intellect

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.349
to explore, to discover, and understand the
cosmos.

